# We Will Not Lose What Is Ours
Tamil title: இழக்க மாட்டோம்

The Tamil language has become known in all these countries. But within India it has not been properly introduced.

The people of Russia and Czechoslovakia have recognised the value of the Tamil language. Here, however, it has not been properly understood.

When we learn that Tamil is being studied at a university in England, and when we remember that we are the inheritors of such a language, our thoughts travel somewhere far away. But when we consider our present condition, our thoughts return to the condition in which we now live.

I said that our thoughts travel to some distant place. We will certainly reach that sacred destination. When? Tamils can surely attain it when they recognise their Tamil culture, understand their history, realise their strength, unite, and work together.

Let us firmly declare that we will use the studies conducted on the Tamil language, and their findings, solely for the growth of Tamil!

Tamil culture accepts all human beings in the world as brothers and comrades. It greets and welcomes those who come to it. It duly respects every language. Wherever in the world the riches of knowledge may be found, it seeks them out and brings them home!

But Tamils will never consent to lose what is their own.

When experts from England and other countries tell us that Tamil is such a precious language, and we remember that we are its inheritors, how could attachment and affection for that language fail to arise?

If such a language is denied its distinct respect at any time, for any purpose, or by anyone at all, the Tamil heart will not tolerate it.

The deep sea is always in turmoil. If one wishes to cross it safely, one can do so in a sound boat, in a ship.

Likewise, if Tamils are embraced and taken along, no people can be better than they.

Even though the United Nations works with unity as its objective, and although the flags of every country fly outside while delegates speak inside and emerge, there will still be discussion of who might invade which country!

Yet our hearts swell whenever we reflect on the magnanimity of the Tamils, who more than a thousand years ago fashioned the true spirit of unity into the golden maxim, "Every place is our town; everyone is our kin."

The poet who sang, "Every place is our town; everyone is our kin," sang in the very next line, "Good and evil do not come from others."

Do we want good to come? We ourselves must bring it about. Do we want evil to come? We ourselves must summon it!

Therefore, do not fear that evil may come. It will come only if we ourselves seek it! Neither evil nor good is given by others.

If we do not want it, it will not come.

Evil does not befall Tamils because others give it to them. Unless we seek evil ourselves, it will never befall the Tamil people.

A time will come when, as we recognise the worth and greatness of Tamil, others too will recognise it and turn to us Tamils, saying:

"This Tamil language does not belong to you alone. It is our language too. It is the language that links us. It is the common language. It is the language of government."

That time will not arrive through a hurried stride, nor will it be gained in a state of haste!

Just as an angler waits patiently until a fish approaches the hook, we must adopt and learn such patience.

Both Tamil scripture and the Tamil way teach that action must be undertaken with knowledge of time, place, manner, and substance.

Following that path, let us create the auspicious day on which we can seat the Tamil language upon the throne!

*At the inauguration of the Second World Tamil Conference*

*Dravida Nadu Pongal Annual, 14 January 1968*

---NOTES---
The surviving source begins without a printed heading, so the Tamil and English titles follow the archive filename and the article's central declaration. The scan resolves the damaged final maxim as காலம் - இடம் - வகை - பொருள் அறிந்து செய்ய வேண்டும். The speech and publication citations are complete.
